<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><title>PIT · Legal & privacy</title></head><body><main class="shell"><header><a class="brand" href="../">pit<span>.</span></a><nav><a href="../">Trade</a><a href="../fees/">Fees</a></nav></header><section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Testnet preview</div><h1>Legal & privacy.</h1></section><section class="panel"><h2>Experimental software</h2><p>PIT is an experimental, non-custodial interface. It does not hold assets or private keys. Transactions are signed by your wallet and submitted to independent protocols. Test assets only in this release. Protocol, smart-contract, liquidation, and execution risks remain.</p><h2>MiCA / PSAN opinion — pending</h2><p>PLACEHOLDER: insert the dated opinion of qualified counsel, jurisdiction analysis, operator identity, registration requirements, eligibility restrictions, and approved terms before any public mainnet launch. This page does not assert regulatory approval or exemption.</p><h2>Tax records</h2><p>CSV files are records for review, not tax advice. Stablecoins remain crypto assets. Perpetual fills export separately because the Taxlot v1 schema has no derivative model. Records are stored only in this browser and can be lost when storage is cleared. Export regularly.</p><h2>Privacy</h2><p>No address analytics or telemetry is configured by PIT. Wallet providers, public RPC hosts, CoW and Hyperliquid receive requests needed for your actions and can observe IP addresses and public wallet addresses. Nothing is sent to a PIT backend; there is no backend.</p><h2>Country restriction stub</h2><p>The country list in configuration and client-side check are non-authoritative. They do not determine your location or establish compliance. Enforce eligibility through a reviewed deployment policy before launch.</p></section><footer><a href="../">Back to trading →</a></footer></main></body></html>
